  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a combination of structured pattern initialization and guided local optimization to systematically explore the solution space while enforcing constraints. Unlike the previous approach's random perturbations, this method leverages known mathematical shapes (sine, triangle, square waves) that inherently balance integral constraints and correlation properties. The critical bottleneck addressed is premature convergence in local optima by using multiple structured initializations and adaptive perturbation strategies.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Use a piecewise constant function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] sampled at n_points points, with h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2.\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx, equivalent to max_l (np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode='full') * dx)[l]\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 exactly\n- Surrogate Strategy: Use a composite function combining sinusoidal and piecewise components to construct initial guesses, then optimize with constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Parent validation:\n      - If initial_h_values exists: scale and clip to meet \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n      - Set n_points = 500 (balance resolution and speed)\n   b. Generate 4 structured initial guesses:\n      1. Sinusoidal: h(x) = 0.5 + A * sin(\u03c0x) with A chosen via normalization\n      2. Square wave: h(x) = 0.5 + 0.5 * sign(sin(\u03c0x))\n      3. Triangle wave: h(x) = 0.5 + 0.5 * |sin(\u03c0x)|\n      4. Gaussian-like: h(x) = exp(-\u03b2(x - 1)^2) with \u03b2 tuned to meet \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n   c. For each initial_guess in [structured guesses + parent]:\n      i. Enforce sum constraint via clipping and scaling\n      ii. Optimize using SLSQP:\n         - Decision vector: h values\n         - Objective: max_correlation * dx\n         - Constraints: \n             - Sum constraint (equality)\n             - Bounds [0,1]\n         - Tolerance: 1e-6\n         - Max iterations: 200\n      iii. Store best solution found\n   d. Perform 100 iterations of:\n      - Random perturbations with amplitude \u00b15% of current h\n      - SLSQP refinement with adaptive tolerance\n      - Track worst-case correlation\n      - Periodic restarts with new structured patterns\n   e. Final validation:\n      - Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n      - Verify \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 within 1e-6\n      - Recompute C\u2085 via full correlation\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   Default configuration:\n      - n_points = 500 (balance resolution and speed)\n      - Initial guesses: 4 structured patterns + parent\n      - Perturbation amplitude: \u00b15% of current h\n      - SLSQP tolerance: 1e-6\n      - Max iterations: 100\n      - Restart frequency: every 25 iterations\n   Key variations:\n      - Increase n_points to 600 for finer resolution\n      - Use simulated annealing for global exploration\n      - Test different wave patterns (exponential, sawtooth)\n      - Adjust perturbation amplitude based on correlation gradient\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Time allocation:\n      - 30% for structured initial guesses (150s)\n      - 50% for optimization iterations (250s)\n      - 20% for final refinement (100s)\n   Robustness measures:\n      - Track best solution in memory\n      - Time-out solver after 20s per iteration\n      - Revert to parent solution if divergence occurs\n      - Fallback: Use parent solution if no improvement after 50 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   Post-optimization checks:\n      - Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] via clipping\n      - Verify \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 within 1e-6\n      - Recompute C\u2085 via full correlation\n      - Compare with parent solution if fallback is needed\n   Output guarantees:\n      - Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n      - h_values: 1D numpy array of floats\n      - c5_bound: float with C\u2085 value\n      - n_points: int indicating sample count\n   Sanity checks:\n      - Values must be in [0,1]\n      - Sum must equal n_points/2\n      - C\u2085 must be finite and calculated correctly\n      - Objectively match the evaluator's computation",
